e1a8e09da19f48b9097c6c820a89af3c5aed","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/clamav-filter/commits/41b4e1a8e09da19f48b9097c6c820a89af3c5aed"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/16238554?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/fbourasse","id":16238554,"login":"fbourasse","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE2MjM4NTU0","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/clamav-filter/commits/f6a65fefc48bc6ee1635900e7811ae375dc916f3/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-21T13:49:03Z","email":"fbourasse@jahia.com","name":"Florent BOURASSÉ"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-21T13:49:03Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix: make the scan gate deny-by-default (SEC-418 scan-coverage residual) (#77)\n\nCloses the SEC-418 scan-coverage residual (https://github.com/Jahia/jahia-security-scan/issues/1614).\n\nThe SEC-141 fix generalised scanning from multipart-only to an allow-list of exactly two\nfurther shapes -- an `application/octet-stream` body, or a PUT with a body -- and passed\neverything else through. Three gaps remained, all reproduced live on 8.2.3.2 and\n8.2.4.0-SNAPSHOT:\n\n  - method gap: the verb test was `\"PUT\".equalsIgnoreCase(...)`, so PATCH and DELETE bodies\n    were never considered;\n  - case gap:   the media-type test was a case-SENSITIVE `startsWith`, while RFC 7231\n    3.1.1.1 makes media types case-insensitive, so `Application/OCTET-STREAM` slipped past;\n  - type gap:   only `application/octet-stream` counted as a binary body, so a POST\n    declaring `application/pdf` or `text/plain` was not scanned at all.\n\n`shouldScan` is now deny-by-default: any request carrying a body is buffered and scanned\nthrough the same fail-closed path, whatever the method and whatever the declared content\ntype, unless its media type is explicitly exempt. Media types are compared\ncase-insensitively with parameters stripped (Locale.ROOT); the HTTP method no longer\ndecides whether an upload is scanned. `mayHaveBody` treats an unknown Content-Length as a\nbody unless the method is GET/HEAD, rather than keying on Transfer-Encoding -- RFC 9113\n8.2.2 forbids that header in HTTP/2, so an h2 streamed upload carries neither and would\nhave been waved through. An empty-buffer short-circuit in `scanBody` is the backstop and\nkeeps bodyless requests off the daemon.\n\nBREAKING CHANGE: the set of scanned requests is much larger.\n\n  Impacted if your deployment sends non-multipart, non-octet-stream bodies through Jahia\n  -- PATCH/DELETE bodies, `text/*`, `application/xml`, image or document content types.\n  These were never scanned before and now are, so they are subject to the module''s\n  existing fail-closed behaviour: infected -> 403, and while the daemon is unreachable ->\n  503 instead of passing through. The multipart provisioning API has been scanned since\n  SEC-141, so module deployment through it also fails while ClamAV is down.\n\n  Not impacted: `application/json` (and any `+json`), `application/x-www-form-urlencoded`,\n  `application/graphql` and `text/x-gwt-rpc` are exempt, so GraphQL, REST, login and the\n  GWT-RPC authoring UI (jContent, Content Manager, Page Composer) behave exactly as\n  before during a ClamAV outage. GraphQL file uploads use multipart and were already\n  scanned. Bodyless requests and GET/HEAD are untouched.\n\n  To upgrade: ensure the ClamAV daemon is reachable and sized for the added traffic\n  before deploying. No configuration change is required -- the exemption list is not\n  configurable by design, so it cannot be widened into a bypass at runtime.\n\n`text/x-gwt-rpc` is on the exemption list because the e2e suite proved its absence\nfail-closes the whole authoring UI: every `POST /gwt/*.gwt` answered 503. Unit tests\ncannot catch that class of mistake -- they assert the rule you designed, not the set of\nmedia types Jahia actually uses.\n\nVerification:\n\n  - 176 unit tests (was 129). Mutation-checked: restoring the pre-SEC-418 predicate in\n    place fails 20 of them, so they detect the vulnerability rather than describe the code.\n  - Live A/B using the fiche''s own reproduction and its own discriminator (the\n    fail-closed log-line delta, never the HTTP status), on 8.2.3.2 with clamd deliberately\n    unreachable: all three positive controls fired, and all five shapes the fiche measured\n    as SCANNED=0 on 1.0.4 are now SCANNED=1. The exemptions and bodyless requests are\n    SCANNED=0. SEC-418 no longer reproduces.\n  - Docker Cypress suite green: 33/33, all 5 specs.\n  - OSGi Import-Package unchanged (the MediaType.*_VALUE constants inline at compile time).\n\nAlso included, both pre-existing and unrelated to the security change:\n\n  - build: the pinned node was raised v22.6.0 -> v22.11.0. The module could not be built\n    at all -- the locked sass-loader@17.0.0 requires node >= 22.11.0 and Yarn Classic\n    treats the engine mismatch as fatal, so `yarn install` aborted before webpack ran.\n  - tests: `cypress/support/bindFetch.js` binds fetch before @jahia/cypress pulls in\n    cross-fetch, whose browser build re-exports the native fetch detached from the window\n    (`exports = ctx.fetch`, browser-ponyfill.js:678). Apollo''s HttpLink called it bare, so\n    every `cy.apollo()` failed with \"Illegal invocation\" -- 13 failures that looked like a\n    broken module. The proper fix belongs upstream in Jahia/jahia-cypress.\n  - tests: provisioning now points the module at the `clamav` service before any spec\n    runs, removing a cross-spec dependency where a failed saveSettings silently degraded\n    403 scan assertions into 503.\n  - tests: ci.startup.sh now forwards \"$@\", so the documented `./ci.startup.sh notests`\n    works instead of running the whole suite.\n\nNote: this repository has no build or test CI workflow (only .github/dependabot.yml plus\norg-level CodeQL), which is why the broken node pin, the broken Apollo client and the\ndropped ci.startup.sh argument all went unnoticed.","tree":{"sha":"098d167a0db08f1f99d4f823b66aa6006dd5084f","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/clamav-filter/git/trees/098d167a0db08f1f99d4f823b66aa6006dd5084f"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/clamav-filter/git/commits/f6a65fefc48bc6ee1635900e7811ae375dc916f3","verification":{"payload":"tree 098d167a0db08f1f99d4f823b66aa6006dd5084f\nparent d974947fae26f942bfec67a06d892324ad426288\nauthor Florent BOURASSÉ <fbourasse@jahia.com> 1789998543 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789998543 +0200\n\nfix: make the scan gate deny-by-default (SEC-418 scan-coverage residual) (#77)\n\nCloses the SEC-418 scan-coverage residual (https://github.com/Jahia/jahia-security-scan/issues/1614).\n\nThe SEC-141 fix generalised scanning from multipart-only to an allow-list of exactly two\nfurther shapes -- an `application/octet-stream` body, or a PUT with a body -- and passed\neverything else through. Three gaps remained, all reproduced live on 8.2.3.2 and\n8.2.4.0-SNAPSHOT:\n\n  - method gap: the verb test was `\"PUT\".equalsIgnoreCase(...)`, so PATCH and DELETE bodies\n    were never considered;\n  - case gap:   the media-type test was a case-SENSITIVE `startsWith`, while RFC 7231\n    3.1.1.1 makes media