ASSE"},"message":"fix: harden ClamAV upload filter against scan bypass and misclassification\n\nSecurity review SECURITY-746.\n\nH1 (HIGH): remove the client-controlled `webflowToken` AV-scan bypass.\nScanning was skipped whenever a `webflowToken` request parameter was\npresent — a value any uploader can set — so malware could be uploaded\nthrough a scanned multipart endpoint simply by adding that field. The\nfilter now scans all multipart uploads; the re-readable request wrapper\nalready makes downstream re-consumption safe.\n\nM1 (MEDIUM): reject oversize uploads up front via declared Content-Length\nbefore buffering any bytes into the heap, as defense-in-depth against\nmemory-amplification DoS. The streaming cap still bounds chunked/under-\ndeclared bodies. Documented the per-instance memory ceiling.\n\nL1 (LOW): classify a mid-stream daemon reply through populateVirusScanResult\ninstead of discarding it as a generic IOException. A signature ClamAV\nreports partway through the stream is now FAILED (logged and blocked as\nmalware, 403) rather than ERROR (503), restoring detection signal.\n\nM2/L2: documented residual risks — the admin-gated outbound-connection\nprimitive (host is not range-restricted because the daemon normally runs\non localhost/LAN) and the non-multipart upload coverage gap.\n\nTests: add JUnit 5 + AssertJ unit tests (40 cases) covering the upload\nscope/size gating, daemon-reply classification, log sanitization, and the\nhost whitelist. SonarQube quality gate: OK (0 new issues, ratings A).\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>","tree":{"sha":"1dcbfe4091f4beb29b8cc5c88c04bfa29a41e460","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/clamav-filter/git/trees/1dcbfe4091f4beb29b8cc5c88c04bfa29a41e460"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/clamav-filter/git/commits/847c687739f56af65e145aa296eca94eba063ff9","verification":{"payload":"tree 1dcbfe4091f4beb29b8cc5c88c04bfa29a41e460\nparent d428772063c32ff0bcd3eeb172d6bab03e8dc9a3\nauthor Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com> 1779892070 +0200\ncommitter Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com> 1779892070 +0200\n\nfix: harden ClamAV upload filter against scan bypass and misclassification\n\nSecurity review SECURITY-746.\n\nH1 (HIGH): remove the client-controlled `webflowToken` AV-scan bypass.\nScanning was skipped whenever a `webflowToken` request parameter was\npresent — a value any uploader can set — so malware could be uploaded\nthrough a scanned multipart endpoint simply by adding that field. The\nfilter now scans all multipart uploads; the re-readable request wrapper\nalready makes downstream re-consumption safe.\n\nM1 (MEDIUM): reject oversize uploads up front via declared Content-Length\nbefore buffering any bytes into the heap, as defense-in-depth against\nmemory-amplification DoS. The streaming cap still bounds chunked/under-\ndeclared bodies. Documented the per-instance memory ceiling.\n\nL1 (LOW): classify a mid-stream daemon reply through populateVirusScanResult\ninstead of discarding it as a generic IOException. A signature ClamAV\nreports partway through the stream is now FAILED (logged and blocked as\nmalware, 403) rather than ERROR (503), restoring detection signal.\n\nM2/L2: documented residual risks — the admin-gated outbound-connection\nprimitive (host is not range-restricted because the daemon normally runs\non localhost/LAN) and the non-multipart upload coverage gap.\n\nTests: add JUnit 5 + AssertJ unit tests (40 cases) covering the upload\nscope/size gating, daemon-reply classification, log sanitization, and the\nhost whitelist. SonarQube quality gate: OK (0 new issues, ratings A).\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>\n","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\niQEzBAABCgAdFiEEtcDrrZ6KblczSEt9UhsGUDwSL3kFAmoW/2YACgkQUhsGUDwS\nL3nOcQgAvJEEEsLpqUYBO0BrGz+tAKYCpH3f2xCxUh76LC+ChBWFHnIlxnWeNETy\ns5vXbkO+jicBVwOO2k8DpFD1scWHFOTam6BbRQYJMjucbIz+kJjDFA8YC6pBBfEg\n9m2MQfLnrPp6s96ah8bzWQluLFp/0HCcH